  "recovery_latency_s": 180.42203497886658,
  "client_view_text": " A message that changes his life forever.\nThe lighthouse keeper, Jack, had been stationed on the rocky coast for five years. He had grown accustomed to the solitude, the rhythmic sound of the waves, and the occasional visit from a passing ship. But on this particular day, something caught his eye. A bottle, half-buried in the sand, caught the morning sun and glinted like a tiny jewel. Jack walked over to investigate, his boots sinking into the damp sand.\n\nAs he picked up the bottle, he noticed it was old, the glass worn and weathered. He carefully removed the cork and peered inside. A piece of paper, yellowed with age, was tied to a small piece of twine. Jack untied the twine and pulled out the paper. Unfurling it, he read the message scrawled on it:\n\n\"Dear stranger, I write to you from the SS Aurora, a ship that sailed from England in 1850. We were bound for Australia, but a storm forced us off course. We were wrecked on these very shores. I, Emily, was the only survivor. I've been living on this island for 20 years, keeping a journal and waiting for rescue. I've"
}